// The room template console_room_9x10 (ADR-0010 §4; read by rv/world/room_template.hpp, which documents the format).
// A rectangular console room of 9.0 x 10.0 m with a 3.2 m ceiling, in the plan's frame: millimetres, +X east, +Y up,
// north = -Z, the origin on the floor at the room's centre; yaws in hundredths of a degree, 0 facing south.
//
//   north (hero wall): hw-w | hero corner | fs01 | hw-e, a decoration socket in each corner
//   west and east walls: seven 0.9 m side-wall sockets each (w01..w07, e01..e07, 1 m apart from north to south) and
//     one 1.8 m door-reserved socket between the fourth and the fifth, which nothing ever occupies (M3's doorways)
//   middle: the circulation band, 1.4 m wide along the room's axis, with a low display case at each edge (dc01, dc02)
//   south: the 1.6 m doorway in the middle, 1.6 x 1.5 m kept clear inside it, a decoration socket on each side
//
// The collection fills the two hero-wall modules first, then the side-wall sockets from north to south, alternating
// west and east, so that it frames the hero wall and grows towards the doorway. The room grows south in 2.0 m steps up
// to 16.0 m; each step moves the south wall (its sockets and the doorway with it) and adds two side-wall sockets on each
// side wall, at the end of the collection's order (26 side-wall sockets at 16 m). Sockets stand 30 mm off the walls
// (the wainscot's rail stands 22 mm proud).
//
// Prefabs are named by manifest id and kind, never by a model path. The modules, the display fixtures and the shelf
// dressing are I3 step 1b's prefabs; the hero corner reuses the test room's furniture.
//
// Version 2 (I3 step 16): the sign is a landscape panel of its own (decor.sign.room.generic, 1.6 x 0.4 m, the test
// room's size), and the decoration is what fits the side-wall sockets (0.9 x 0.4 m): a poster, a slim floor lamp, a
// narrow cabinet and a plant (version 1's floor lamp, 0.44 m deep, and sideboard, 0.5 m deep, fitted no socket).
